      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A useful way of representing electronic configurations is a diagram that places electrons in boxes. Each box represents an atomic orbital; the boxes are arranged in order of increasing energy from bottom to top; an electron is an arrow whose direction shows its spin; and when two electrons occupy an orbital their spins are opposite, so the two arrows in a box point in opposite directions. Electrons in the same region of space repel each other because they have the same charge, so wherever possible electrons occupy separate orbitals of the same subshell to minimise the repulsion, with the same spins in different orbitals; they pair only when no empty orbital is left within the subshell. The electronic structures of carbon, nitrogen and oxygen illustrate this: carbon's two 2p electrons and nitrogen's three each occupy separate orbitals, and only in oxygen is a fourth electron forced to pair."
          },
          {
